WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.894
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.894 --> 00:00:09.389
As the last notes of my
violin fell upon the crowd,

00:00:09.389 --> 00:00:12.720
the audience burst into applause.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:18.432
I had just finished the best violin
performance in my life today and

00:00:18.432 --> 00:00:20.477
I was feeling awesome.

00:00:20.477 --> 00:00:26.910
I ran off the stage, up to my mom,
expecting praise, compliments, hugs.

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:33.047
Instead, I was greeted with an unenthused
high five, and a, see Helen,

00:00:33.047 --> 00:00:37.361
if you just apply yourself like
this to other aspects of your life,

00:00:37.361 --> 00:00:42.559
think about how awesome you could be.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:42.559 --> 00:00:43.807
&gt;&gt; But that hurt,

00:00:43.807 --> 00:00:47.170
because her opinion mattered a lot to me.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:53.093
And I'm not sharing the story with you,
to ask for your pity or because I enjoy

00:00:53.093 --> 00:00:56.377
throwing my poor mom under the bus.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:56.377 --> 00:00:58.535
&gt;&gt; But because I can trace a lot of

00:00:58.535 --> 00:01:02.245
my inability to give or
receive good compliments,

00:01:02.245 --> 00:01:05.540
to my strict traditional Chinese heritage.

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:10.852
But you don't have to come from the same
upbringing to maybe also struggle

00:01:10.852 --> 00:01:13.103
with giving or receiving compliments.

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:17.157
And because I'm an ex-consultant,
I'm going to have you guys take a look at

00:01:17.157 --> 00:01:19.310
a chart.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:21.827
&gt;&gt; Take a look at this chart, and

00:01:21.827 --> 00:01:27.276
take a moment and think about maybe
where you fall on this chart.

00:01:27.276 --> 00:01:31.438
I'm willing to bet, if I took a poll,
most of you guys would say,

00:01:31.438 --> 00:01:33.500
[SOUND] somewhere in the middle.

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:39.667
But I'm also willing to bet a lot
of us actually unknowingly fall

00:01:39.667 --> 00:01:42.397
into this bottom left-hand corner.

00:01:42.397 --> 00:01:46.970
Somewhere between not being so
great at giving compliments and

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:49.750
not being so great at receiving them.

00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:54.100
And today, I'm here to not only get us
up to that upper right-hand corner,

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:55.566
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:01:55.566 --> 00:01:58.857
&gt;&gt; But to also drive home the importance

00:01:58.857 --> 00:02:03.960
of being in that corner starting
with giving compliments

00:02:06.144 --> 00:02:08.300
Think about the last time you
gave someone a compliment.

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:12.111
What did you say?

00:02:12.111 --> 00:02:15.088
How specific or
genuine do you think it was?

00:02:15.088 --> 00:02:19.113
When was it?

00:02:19.113 --> 00:02:22.230
I ask these questions because a lot of us,
again,

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:25.360
don't realize how few
good compliments we give.

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:28.734
And there's a lot of good reasons for
that.

00:02:28.734 --> 00:02:32.961
For example, thanks to the illusion of
transparency, we believe that other

00:02:32.961 --> 00:02:36.810
people know how we think, or
know what we feel, and that's not true.

00:02:38.489 --> 00:02:41.172
Other times, we're just trying
to avoid being awkward, so

00:02:41.172 --> 00:02:42.550
we don't say anything at all.

00:02:44.048 --> 00:02:48.050
And even worse,
sometimes we're a little self-absorbed.

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:52.835
And so, we forget about the people
around us as we really focus on what

00:02:52.835 --> 00:02:56.560
we're trying to get done,
what we're trying to do.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:00.647
But stick with me here, because there's
really good reasons as to why we need to

00:03:00.647 --> 00:03:02.300
become better complimenters.

00:03:03.770 --> 00:03:08.179
Starting with the fact that giving
a good compliment actually drives

00:03:08.179 --> 00:03:10.925
achievement at a very fundamental level.

00:03:10.925 --> 00:03:16.350
Researchers in Japan recently discovered
that giving someone a good compliment is

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:20.769
equivalent to giving them straight cash.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:03:20.769 --> 00:03:21.270
&gt;&gt; And yes,

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:23.935
I'm a poor grad student right now so
I love money.

00:03:23.935 --> 00:03:24.543
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:03:24.543 --> 00:03:28.033
&gt;&gt; But this actually, like any reward,

00:03:28.033 --> 00:03:30.410
leaves us wanting more.

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:34.979
It drives us to want to recreate
our stellar performance.

00:03:34.979 --> 00:03:39.300
Giving an awesome compliment is also
better than giving constructive criticism.

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:43.481
This coming from a researcher
at the University of Hamburg.

00:03:43.481 --> 00:03:49.390
Where he noted that giving a good
compliment exacerbates our optimism bias.

00:03:49.390 --> 00:03:54.051
We have a feeling and we believe that good
things will happen and bad things won't.

00:03:54.051 --> 00:04:01.260
[LAUGH] So, when we give a good compliment
or we receive a good compliment.

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:04.361
We then believe that we
can actually achieve and

00:04:04.361 --> 00:04:07.937
do something that maybe we
wouldn't have otherwise.

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:13.502
And finally,
giving a good compliment lifts moods,

00:04:13.502 --> 00:04:17.946
it builds trust, and
it leads to stronger relationships.

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:24.430
And the simple act of just
recognizing those around you,

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:28.590
builds the foundations of reciprocity and
positivity.

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:31.530
That leads to long lasting relationships.

00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:36.660
With all that being said in order to
actually achieve all of these benefits you

00:04:36.660 --> 00:04:38.388
actually have to give a good compliment.

00:04:38.388 --> 00:04:41.380
So what's a good compliment?

00:04:41.380 --> 00:04:47.722
It comes down to giving the content and
having great delivery.

00:04:47.722 --> 00:04:51.030
When it comes down to content,
we're thinking about being specific.

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:56.018
What exactly does someone do
to deserve that compliment?

00:04:56.018 --> 00:04:59.396
When we're specific, we avoid
comparing people to each other, and

00:04:59.396 --> 00:05:01.910
we also show that we were
really paying attention.

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:05.750
We also want to be achievement focused.

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:09.666
We want to think about what they
achieved versus what someone is.

00:05:09.666 --> 00:05:13.589
Because when we think about achievement
and we talk about achievement,

00:05:13.589 --> 00:05:17.010
it's something someone can repeat and
someone can do better.

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:21.849
It's what triggers that
reward center in their brain.

00:05:21.849 --> 00:05:27.090
Now when it comes to delivery, what we
want to talk about is being genuine.

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:31.958
And the easiest way to do that is
to just recognize the impact that

00:05:31.958 --> 00:05:33.503
someone had on you.

00:05:33.503 --> 00:05:36.390
And finally, we want to be selective.

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:41.094
As much as you guys might walk away from
this talk saying you get a compliment, you

00:05:41.094 --> 00:05:45.370
get a compliment, you get a compliment.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:47.637
&gt;&gt; We want to avoid doing that,

00:05:47.637 --> 00:05:51.380
because when you give
too many compliments,

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:55.825
that compliment and
that sincerity, gets diluted.

00:05:55.825 --> 00:05:59.810
Okay, so now that you guys are all
awesome at giving compliments.

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:03.022
Walk with me to the other side,
as we talk about receiving compliments.

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:09.845
When was the last time someone
gave you a compliment?

00:06:09.845 --> 00:06:12.579
How did you react?

00:06:12.579 --> 00:06:16.940
Was it potentially with a question mark,
like [SOUND] thanks?

00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:22.416
Was it maybe with a no, no,
no it wasn't me it was the team.

00:06:22.416 --> 00:06:23.820
It was the weather.

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:26.516
It was luck.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:06:26.516 --> 00:06:27.879
&gt;&gt; These reactions are so

00:06:27.879 --> 00:06:32.329
common that sociolinguists have
categorized them into two categories,

00:06:32.329 --> 00:06:34.085
rejection and deflection.

00:06:34.085 --> 00:06:37.360
And there is a good reason why,
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:40.570
&gt;&gt; We also tend to dodge, dip, duck, dive,

00:06:40.570 --> 00:06:44.777
and dodge compliments like
we're playing dodgeball.

00:06:44.777 --> 00:06:45.651
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:06:45.651 --> 00:06:46.358
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] And

00:06:46.358 --> 00:06:49.103
it all starts from when we were kids.

00:06:49.103 --> 00:06:54.350
We're told that good people and
strong leaders are humble.

00:06:54.350 --> 00:06:58.204
So we don't want to come off as stuck up.

00:06:58.204 --> 00:07:01.650
We also have a tendency to
run away from the spotlight.

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:05.205
So when someone shines the bright
light of praise on us,

00:07:05.205 --> 00:07:07.409
our first reaction is to run away.

00:07:07.409 --> 00:07:10.445
And finally, especially in this room,

00:07:10.445 --> 00:07:16.200
we all tend to be a little self-critical,
some of us very self-critical.

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:19.367
And when someone gives us a compliment
we view it with skepticism.

00:07:19.367 --> 00:07:21.210
What did we even do to deserve it?

00:07:22.630 --> 00:07:28.587
And this last part is really important,
because when we dodge a good compliment.

00:07:28.587 --> 00:07:32.740
We're actually devaluing the perspective,
the time,

00:07:32.740 --> 00:07:38.000
the heart, that someone put
into giving us that compliment.

00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:42.145
And just as much as giving good
compliments builds up relationships,

00:07:42.145 --> 00:07:45.810
dodging good compliments breaks
those relationships down.

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:49.114
Additionally, when we dodge
compliments we're also

00:07:49.114 --> 00:07:52.790
missing out on an awesome
opportunity to better ourselves.

00:07:54.010 --> 00:07:57.233
We did something to really
deserve that compliment.

00:07:57.233 --> 00:08:02.269
Figuring out what that is will make
us better and stronger, faster.

00:08:03.450 --> 00:08:08.005
So when it comes to receiving compliments,
there's really just two pieces.

00:08:08.005 --> 00:08:11.500
Acknowledge, just say thank you.

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:17.413
You're going to want to say more,
but just stop at thank you.

00:08:17.413 --> 00:08:22.890
Internalize, really think about what
you did to deserve that compliment.

00:08:22.890 --> 00:08:27.522
And how you could recreate that, and how
you could apply it elsewhere in your life.

00:08:29.743 --> 00:08:34.721
Now admittedly as I stand in front of you
giving you all this sage advice on giving

00:08:34.721 --> 00:08:36.890
compliments and receiving them.

00:08:36.890 --> 00:08:42.203
I myself continue to really struggle with
becoming a better compliment giver and

00:08:42.203 --> 00:08:43.850
receiver, it's hard.

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:50.293
But just the other day, I was
teaching my seven year old nephew how

00:08:50.293 --> 00:08:56.693
to throw a frisbee, and memories of that
violin recital came flooding back to me.

00:08:56.693 --> 00:09:00.510
And I wondered what I would have
wanted my mom to say in that moment.

00:09:02.390 --> 00:09:07.498
And I was reminded that this was my
opportunity to not repeat history.

00:09:07.498 --> 00:09:12.548
So I countered each of his throws with
a very specific targeted compliment.

00:09:12.548 --> 00:09:17.923
And I watched as his confidence grew,
I saw it in his eyes,

00:09:17.923 --> 00:09:24.200
and to think that I could have that
kind of an impact on a young child.

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:29.285
Was a stark reminder that my work,
that our work in becoming

00:09:29.285 --> 00:09:34.079
better compliment givers and
receivers is never done.

00:09:36.888 --> 00:09:43.231
Thank you.
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:43.231 --> 00:09:46.954
[MUSIC]

